1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Welcome to this short course, Building Applications with Vector Databases,

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.000
built in partnership with Pinecone.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Vector databases have become a key part of the infrastructure stack

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.000
for building applications with large language models,

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.000
specifically RAG or Retrieval Augmented Generation.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.000
But it turns out that they're useful for even more applications

7
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.000
than is widely appreciated.

8
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.000
In this course, you'll learn how to use vector DBs

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.000
to quickly build applications in text similarity search,

10
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.000
RAG, image similarity search, anomaly detection,

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.000
recommended systems, and hybrid search,

12
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.000
which refers to using simultaneously sparse and dense vectors.

13
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.000
I know that's a lot of stuff,

14
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
but that's why I hope you find this course useful.

15
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.000
We'll cover a huge space of applications

16
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.000
that you'll be able to build using vector DBs.

17
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.000
I'm thrilled that the instructor for this course is Tim Tully,

18
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.000
a board member at Pinecone.

19
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.000
For over a decade, Tim had led teams of thousands of engineers

20
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.000
as Spunk's CTO and then Yahoo's Vice President of Engineering.

21
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Tim had also developed the Pinecone vector databases

22
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.000
combined line interface,

23
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.000
and I look forward to him sharing with us

24
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.000
what he's been seeing from working with many people

25
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000
building on top of Pinecone's vector DBs.

26
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.000
Thanks, Andrew. I'm excited to be here.

27
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.000
I know I gave a long list of applications just now,

28
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.000
but let me unpack briefly some of the key topics.

29
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Vector DBs lets you input a collection of vectors,

30
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.000
say embeddings of documents if you're implementing RAG,

31
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.000
and then send queries to it,

32
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.000
such as an embedding of a new text query

33
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.000
to retrieve documents relevant to your query.

34
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
That's what made vector DBs a key component of RAG

35
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.000
where you fetch additional context for an LM

36
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.000
to generate this response.

37
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.000
But it turns out that this ability to fetch similar vectors

38
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.000
makes it very useful for many other applications as well.

39
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.000
For example, in image similarity search,

40
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.000
you can compute embeddings of images,

41
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.000
and then use the vector DB to quickly find similar images.

42
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.000
Or you can carry out anomaly detection

43
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.000
by checking if a new item has anything similar to it,

44
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.000
and if it doesn't, then maybe it's an anomaly.

45
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.000
There are a lot of details on how to implement these effectively,

46
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.000
and Tim will dive into the specific mental frameworks

47
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.000
and tips for building these applications.

48
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Yes, as Andrew said,

49
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.000
there are a lot of potential applications for vector databases.

50
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.000
You'll learn about six of them in this course.

51
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.000
First, you'll start by building a basic semantic search

52
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.000
for text documents.

53
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Then, you'll move into building a RAG app.

54
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:43.000
In the next step, you'll build a recommender system.

55
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Next, you will implement a hybrid search app

56
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.000
for product recommendation.

57
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.000
Then, you will build a child-parent image similarity app.

58
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.000
And finally, you will work on an anomaly detection app

59
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.000
based on a dataset of server logs.

60
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.000
Through these examples,

61
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.000
you'll learn how to use a vector database

62
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.000
to store and help you manipulate lots of different datasets.

63
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.000
We'll draw from examples from Wikipedia text,

64
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.000
pictures of faces, Quora text,

65
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.000
structured data in the form of network logs,

66
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.000
and a fashion dataset with paired images and text.

67
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.000
One of the cool techniques that you'll learn about,

68
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.000
as well, is hybrid search,

69
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.000
in which you can use a vector database

70
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.000
to manipulate vectors with both a sparse and a dense component.

71
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.000
For example, in the product recommendation setting,

72
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.000
you'll see how to use a dense embedding of images of clothing

73
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.000
alongside a sparse embedding of a text description

74
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.000
and how to dial a knob controlling the relative weighting

75
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.000
between the sparse and dense parts of the vector.

76
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.000
By learning the foundations of vector databases

77
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.000
and working on the applications you'll learn in this course,

78
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.000
I'm sure you'll find even more possibilities to explore.

79
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Many people have worked to create this course.

80
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.000
I'd like to thank, on the Pinecone side,

81
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.000
James Briggs, Ram Sriharsha, and Bear Douglas.

82
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.000
From DeepLearning.ai,

83
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.000
Diala Ezzeddine and Esmaeil Gargari

84
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.000
have also contributed to this course.

85
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.000
In the first lesson,

86
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.000
you will explore the essentials of semantic search,

87
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.000
which will be used throughout the other lessons in this course.

88
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.000
That sounds great,

89
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.000
and a lot of materials in this one short course.

90
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.000
So, let's go add a new dimension

91
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.000
to what we can do using vector DBs.

92
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.000
Let's go on to the next video and get started.

